kara_support: FYI escalated ticket from a customer on Vercel edge runtime. They report the ReadableStream closes after ~2-4s mid-response. Happens consistently when session token is rotated by our short-lived credential service. Attaching minimal repro and a couple of logs. @sam_eng-runtime can you take a look? :eyes:

sam_eng-runtime: Thanks Kara. Can you paste the SDK snippet they're using and the exact SDK version? Also need the request-id from the failing stream response.

kara_support: Customer says they're on @redwood/js v0.9.2 (edge build). Snippet they sent:```ts
import Redwood from '@redwood/sdk';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_TOKEN });
const res = await client.stream({ model: 'open-8b', input: 'stream me' });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
while(true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(value));
}
```
They say the loop breaks with done=true and then no more tokens, but server kept generating (they verified via server debug).

sam_eng-runtime: That pattern looks fine. Two quick hypotheses: 1) the fetch/polyfill in some edge runtimes emits done when it receives a non-200 mid-stream (our SDK treats a 401/403 on refresh as terminal and calls controller.terminate), 2) a token-swap callback in the SDK is invoking response.close prematurely in v0.9.2. There's a known fix in 0.9.4 where we avoid calling stream.cancel() during async auth flows — can you try upgrading to 0.9.4 in the repro?

leo_devex: If they can't upgrade immediately, tell them a workaround to avoid using the SDK auto-refresh path and instead pass a long-lived token (or refresh outside loop). Also a local workaround is to read the stream via async iteration on the raw body, not the SDK's wrapped stream:```ts
const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
const reader = res.body!.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
while (true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  process.stdout.write(decoder.decode(value));
}
```
That keeps you in control if a token refresh happens elsewhere.

jordan_support-eng: Kara, can you drop the server-side logs where they show the session token refresh flow? We want the request-id and any 401/403 emitted around the time of the early close.

kara_support: Pasted logs (sanitized):```
[2026-03-16T18:02:10Z] req=rid:0a3f client=customer edge=nyc-1 stream-start model=open-8b
[2026-03-16T18:02:12Z] req=rid:0a3f auth=rotate token_id=st-7a2 exp=2026-03-16T18:02:12Z
[2026-03-16T18:02:12Z] req=rid:0a3f auth=refresh-status 200
[2026-03-16T18:02:13Z] req=rid:0a3f downstream=origin status=200 chunk=125
[2026-03-16T18:02:14Z] req=rid:0a3f auth=rotate token_id=st-7a3 exp=2026-03-16T18:02:14Z
[2026-03-16T18:02:14Z] req=rid:0a3f auth=refresh-status 401 (unexpected)
[2026-03-16T18:02:14Z] req=rid:0a3f stream=terminated by-sdk (client side)
```
They flagged the 401 during a token swap as correlated with the immediate client-side stream termination.

sam_eng-runtime: That 401 during a swap is exactly the scenario fixed in 0.9.4. In v0.9.2 the SDK's auth refresh raced and threw, which triggered our stream cleanup path — this resolves as a race condition. Two immediate actions: 1) upgrade to 0.9.4 (canary rolled to prod shortly), 2) advise the customer to use a non-auto-rotating token until they upgrade. We'll patch the edge runtime to swallow transient 401s during active streaming to avoid cascading closes.

ops_bot: deploy:patch v0.9.4 -> canary applied to edge-northamerica cluster (deploy id: dply-20260316-09). :white_check_mark:

leo_devex: Also include this tiny client-side defensive pattern if they want to avoid missing partial output: instead of assuming done=true means terminal, keep a short reconnect/backoff, requesting the remainder via a resume cursor (if their app protocol supports). Example naive reconnect strategy:```ts
// pseudo-code: keep partial output, attempt reconnect on unexpected done=true
let buffer = '';
while(true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) {
    // try quick reconnect once
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 300));
    const retry = await fetch(retryUrl, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
    if (retry.ok) {
      // resume logic...
    } else break;
  }
  buffer += decoder.decode(value || new Uint8Array());
}
```
Not ideal but reduces user-facing interruptions.

jordan_support-eng: Kara — I'll message the customer with the upgrade path and the temporary token guidance. If they can't update we can toggle the edge canary to their region to see if the canary behavior removes the early close. Please collect the customer cluster/region and a short window where it occurred.

kara_support: Got it. I've told them to try v0.9.4 and switch tokens. Customer will test in the next hour and report back with request-id if it persists. Thanks all. :pray:
